β. strí woman, originally a disyllable, is inflected as a radical monosyllabic stem in the sing. A. and pl. N.A.I.: stríy-am; stríy-as, strī-bhís (accent); but retains traces of its derivative origin in the s. N. strí (no s), D. striy-ái² (AV.), G. striy-ás, L. striy-ám (AV.).

II. The ū declension, which comprises both radical and derivative stems, is much more homogeneous than the ī declension. The inflexion of these two classes corresponds exactly to that of the two divisions of the radical ī declension. Practically all the stems in this declension are oxytones (including both the compound radical and the derivative stems).

a. In the radical class there are seven monosyllabic stems, five of which are f.: dū gift, bhū earth, brū brow, syū thread, srū stream; one m. and f.: sú begetter and mother; one m.: jū speeding, steed. There are further two reduplicated f. substantives and one adjective: juhū tongue, juhū sacrificial spoon; jógū singing aloud. Finally, there are about sixty compounds, almost exclusively formed from about eleven roots, e. g. pari-bhū surrounding.

