*6. Roots* liable to Samprasāraṇa (17 note 1) take it; e. g. vac speak: uc-yá-te; vad speak: ud-yá-te; vah carry: uh-yá-te; grah seize: grh-yá-te.

a. Derivative verbs in aya (causatives) drop the suffix while retaining the strong radical vowel. Only one such stem has been noted in the Saṃhitās: bhāj-yá-te is caused to share (from bhāj-áya causative of bhaj share).

a. The forms of the present indicative passive occurring, if made from hū call, would be:

Sing. 1\. hū-yé. 2\. hū-yá-se. 3\. hū-yá-te. Du. 3\. hū-yé-te. Pl. 1\. hū-yá-mahe. 3\. hū-yá-nte.

b. As regards the moods only two certain subjunctive forms (s. 3\. uh-yá-te, bhri-yá-te) and one injunctive (s. 3\. sū-ya-ta: sū bring forth) occur. No optative forms occur in the RV. or AV.¹ There are, however, nearly thirty imperative forms occurring in the 2\. 3\. s. pl. These forms, illustrated by hū call, are: s. 2\. hū-yá-sva. 3\. hū-yá-tām. Pl. 2\. hū-yá-dhvam. 3\. hū-yá-ntām.

c. Of the participle over forty examples occur; e. g. hū-yá-māna being called. Of the imperfect only about eight forms have been noted, occurring only in the 3\. s. and pl.: á-hū-ya-ta and á-hū-yanta.


