1\. ná, which is taken by primary verbs only, is attached to the (unweakened) root, which ends in a long vowel or one of the consonants d and (rarely) c or j. Before this suffix, ī and ū remain unchanged; ā remains or is reduced to ī or i; ȳ becomes īr or (generally when a labial precedes) ūr; d is assimilated to n; c and j revert to the original guttural. Thus lī cling: lī-ná; dū burn: dū-ná; drā sleep: drā-ṇá; dā divide: di-ná; hā leave: hī-ná; gȳ swallow: gīr-ṇá; mȳ crush: mūr-ṇá; jȳ waste away: jūr-ṇá; bhid split: bhin-ná; skand leap: skan-ná; vraśc cut up: vȳk-ṇá; ruj break: rug-ṇá.

