β. The first of two antithetical clauses is frequently accented,¹ especially when the antithesis is clearly indicated by corresponding words like anyá-anyá, éka-éka, ca-ca, vā-vā; e. g. prá-prḁ anyé yánti, páry anyá āsate while some go on, others sit down; sám cḁ idhásva agne, prá ca bodhayḁ enam both be kindled, Agni, and waken this man's knowledge. When the verb of two such clauses is the same, it usually appears (accented) in the first only; e. g. dvipāc ca sárvaṃ no rákṣa, cátuspād yác ca naḥ svám protect both every biped of ours and whatever quadruped is our own.

γ. The verb of the second clause is accented if it is a 1\. pers. subjunctive or 2\. pers. imperative² with a final meaning, and the verb of the first clause is a 2\. pers. impv. of ā+i, gam, or yā go; e. g. éta, dhíyaṃ kṛṇávāma come, we will offer prayer; tūyam ā gahi, káṇveṣu sú sácā píba come quickly, beside the Kaṇvas drink thy fill. In B. the verb of the first clause is an impv. of either ā-i or prá-i; e. g. éhi̥ idám pátāva (ŚB.) come, we will now fly thither; préta tád eṣyámo yátrḁ imám ásurā vibhájante come, we will go thither where the Asuras are dividing this earth (ŚB.). The second verb is, however, in similar passages not infrequently left unaccented in B.


